{
 "id": "2026-belaroussi-sidewalk",
 "name": "Sidewalk Audit: Walking Needs and Well-being (Santa Cruz de Tenerife)",
 "description": "Method replication: same task, statements and design as the paper; the images are not the paper's stimuli (new Google Street View views of the same three streets, © Google). Belaroussi & Salingaros (2026, AI 7:110) asked whether ChatGPT and Gemini can stand in for people when auditing a sidewalk. 68 people (mostly researchers and staff, none living in the area) saw three sidewalks in Santa Cruz de Tenerife: Calle San Martín (residential), Calle Serís (commercial) and Calle Méndez Núñez (by the City Hall). For each photo they first imagined walking on the sidewalk and rated four walking-need statements on a 5-point agreement scale (pleasurability, comfort, safety from traffic, accessibility) and gave an overall pedestrian-friendly score out of 10; then they imagined standing there and rated two well-being statements (place: a pleasure to wait here; distance: the distant view draws me on). No definitions were given. The LLMs got the same seven items 10 times each. Analysis as in the paper: per statement and sidewalk, the median and interquartile range of human answers, then the median difference and intraclass correlation against any AI ratings you collect with the same wording. Differences from the original study: (1) the photos are Google Street View views (2026) taken on the same three streets, chosen to match the paper's descriptions (yellow curb line on San Martín, palms and parked scooters on Imeldo Serís, the old building wall and bike lane on Méndez Núñez); the paper used the authors' smartphone photos, available only on request; (2) the order of the three sidewalks is randomised per participant; (3) the friendliness score uses buttons 1–10; (4) the question \"Have you ever lived in Santa Cruz de Tenerife?\" is added because the paper only recruited people who had not.",
